.lua
book.chapter(29, "Parser combinators in MetaML")
.endlua
.sh 2 "Parsers are typed values"
.pp
Parsec exposes opaque Parser values parameterized by the result they produce.
A string parser returns String, a sequence can return a tuple, and a mapped
parser can return a domain constructor. This makes grammar assembly ordinary
typed programming. Combinators express sequencing, alternatives, repetition,
and semantic conversion without requiring a separate grammar-action language.
The host module must be installed and permitted in an embedding before its
parser handles can be used.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[words := Parsec.sep_by (Parsec.regex "[a-z]+") (Parsec.char ",")
Test.equal "separated words" (Parsec.run words "a,bc") (Ok ["a", "bc"])
Test.equal "empty list" (Parsec.run words "") (Ok [])
Test.equal "prefix interface" (Parsec.run_prefix (Parsec.string "ab") "abc")
  (Ok ("ab", "c"))
Test.check "complete interface" (Result.is_err (Parsec.run (Parsec.string "ab") "abc"))]=],
    "Complete parsing and prefix parsing are distinct contracts")
.endlua
.pp
Run requires complete consumption. Run_prefix returns the remaining suffix
alongside the parsed value. Choose explicitly: a command protocol may parse
one frame at a time, while a configuration parser should reject unexplained
trailing input. It is a common integration error to test only valid input,
then discover that the production caller accidentally selected the prefix
interface and ignored the remainder.
.sh 2 "Sequence retains the results you need"
.pp
Seq returns a pair of parsed results. Left and right sequence two parsers
while keeping only the selected side. Between keeps the middle result and
discards delimiters. These operations let a grammar recognize punctuation
without forcing every semantic handler to remove punctuation manually.
Token handles a convenient whitespace convention, while spaces provides an
explicit whitespace parser. Keep that convention consistent across the grammar.
.lua
book.code("metaml", [=[name := Parsec.regex "[a-z]+"
wrapped := Parsec.between (Parsec.char "(") (Parsec.char ")") name
Test.equal "discard delimiters" (Parsec.run wrapped "(abc)") (Ok "abc")
pair := Parsec.seq (Parsec.string "ab") (Parsec.string "cd")
Test.equal "retain both" (Parsec.run pair "abcd") (Ok ("ab", "cd"))
number := Parsec.map Int.from_string (Parsec.regex "[0-9]+")
Test.equal "mapped result" (Parsec.run number "42") (Ok (Some 42))]=],
    "Parser structure and semantic result structure")
.endlua
.pp
The last parser returns an Option inside Result because lexical recognition
and integer conversion are separate operations. That nesting is honest:
matching digits does not prove representability as a signed integer. A
larger parser can use bind to turn conversion failure into parser failure,
or preserve a separate validation stage. Avoid Option.get merely to make
the apparent result type shorter when malformed numeric input is expected.
.sh 2 "Alternatives and diagnostics"
.pp
Alt chooses between parsers and choice accepts a list of alternatives. Parser
failures backtrack in this MetaML host interface, with error reporting tracking
a furthest failure position. Label associates a useful expectation with an
operation. These contracts should not be confused with the native DSLtk
combinator core's committed-failure rules, discussed in chapter 36. Related
interfaces can deliberately expose different control over backtracking.
.pp
Order alternatives from the intended grammar, then test shared prefixes.
Repeated backtracking across long common prefixes can multiply work even
when the grammar looks short. Factoring a common prefix often improves both
performance and diagnostics because the parser makes the shared decision
once. Error labels should describe a language construct, such as an operand,
rather than expose an implementation detail such as a particular helper name.
.sh 2 "Repetition must make progress"
.pp
Many permits zero or more results; many1 requires at least one. Optional
wraps a possible result, and sep_by handles delimiter-separated sequences.
Never repeat a parser that can succeed without consuming input. A safe
implementation detects nonprogress, but the grammar is still suspect: there
is no finite number of empty successes determined by the source. Review
nullable parsers before putting them beneath a repetition combinator.
.lua
book.algorithm("Safe repetition", {
    "Save the cursor position before attempting the element parser.",
    "If the element fails according to the repetition contract, stop or propagate.",
    "If it succeeds without advancing, report nonprogress.",
    "Otherwise append its value and attempt the next element.",
    "Return results in source order."
})
.endlua
.pp
This rule also applies indirectly. A parser built from optional or pure may
succeed empty even though its name suggests a meaningful element. Track
nullability as a property of grammar components, not just of primitive
parsers. Empty input, a trailing separator, and an empty delimited group
are important tests because they exercise these boundaries without requiring
a large generated corpus.
.sh 2 "Recursive construction needs laziness"
.pp
Parsec.lazy accepts a function that supplies a parser later. This breaks
recursive parser construction: building a parenthesized expression parser
must not immediately build itself forever before any source is read.
Construction laziness is different from allowing direct left recursion
during parsing. A recursive-descent grammar must consume input before
re-entering the same recursive path or use a different parsing strategy.
.pp
Separate parsing, lowering, validation, and execution when their failure
models differ. A parser can produce a generic tree or a lightweight syntax
ADT, a validator can resolve names and check arities, and an evaluator can
operate on established invariants. This arrangement yields smaller semantic
callbacks and more useful tests than embedding every domain action inside
backtracking parser functions, where an attempted branch might run repeatedly.
.sh 2 "Sources and further work"
.pp
Read metalib/cpp/Parsec.h and its implementation fragment, plus the D mirror.
Construct a parser for parenthesized comma-separated identifiers and specify
whether an empty group is legal. Test trailing commas, missing closing
delimiters, and extra suffixes. Then add integer payloads with explicit
range validation. Explain which failures arise from syntax, which arise
from numeric representation, and which parser components can accept empty input.
